---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Glade — A Visual Environment for Bayesian Workflow Analysis">
  <style>
    @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=Source+Sans+3:wght@400;700&display=swap');

    :root {
      --paper: #fefefe;
      --ink: #000000;
      --rule: #000000;
      --blue: #0055aa;
      --dim: #444444;
    }

    * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }

    body {
      font-family: 'Computer Modern Serif', 'Latin Modern Roman', 'Times New Roman', serif;
      background: var(--paper);
      color: var(--ink);
      line-height: 1.45;
      -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
    }

    .paper {
      max-width: 680px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 48px 48px 80px;
    }

    /* Two-column header */
    .header {
      display: flex;
      justify-content: space-between;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 24px;
      padding-bottom: 12px;
    }

    .header-left { flex: 1; }

    .header-right {
      text-align: right;
      font-size: 12px;
      color: var(--dim);
      line-height: 1.6;
      white-space: nowrap;
      min-width: 140px;
    }

    .paper-title {
      font-size: 18px;
      font-weight: normal;
      text-align: center;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
      line-height: 1.3;
    }

    .paper-title em {
      font-style: italic;
    }

    .authors {
      text-align: center;
      font-size: 14px;
      margin-bottom: 4px;
    }

    .affiliation {
      text-align: center;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--dim);
      margin-bottom: 20px;
    }

    .abstract {
      margin: 0 40px 24px;
      padding: 12px 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    }

    .abstract-label {
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: normal;
      text-align: center;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
      font-style: italic;
    }

    .abstract p {
      font-size: 13px;
      text-align: justify;
      text-indent: 1.5em;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }

    .abstract p:first-of-type {
      text-indent: 0;
    }

    .keywords {
      font-size: 12px;
      text-align: center;
      color: var(--dim);
      margin-bottom: 28px;
    }

    .keywords strong {
      font-weight: normal;
      font-style: italic;
    }

    /* Section headings */
    .section-heading {
      font-size: 14px;
      font-weight: normal;
      text-align: center;
      margin: 32px 0 16px;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    }

    .subsection-heading {
      font-size: 13px;
      font-style: italic;
      margin: 24px 0 8px;
    }

    /* Body text */
    .body-text {
      font-size: 13px;
      text-align: justify;
      text-indent: 1.5em;
      margin-bottom: 8px;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }

    .body-text:first-of-type {
      text-indent: 0;
    }

    /* Equations */
    .equation {
      text-align: center;
      margin: 16px 0;
      font-style: italic;
      font-size: 14px;
    }

    .equation-number {
      float: right;
      margin-top: -20px;
    }

    /* Figure */
    .figure {
      margin: 20px auto;
      max-width: 520px;
    }

    .figure-box {
      border: 1px solid #999;
      padding: 20px;
    }

    .figure-caption {
      font-size: 11px;
      text-align: center;
      margin-top: 8px;
      line-height: 1.4;
    }

    .figure-caption .fig-label {
      font-weight: normal;
    }

    /* Workflow diagram */
    .workflow {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      gap: 4px;
      padding: 8px 0;
      font-size: 11px;
      font-family: 'Computer Modern Sans', sans-serif;
    }

    .wf-step {
      padding: 6px 12px;
      border: 1px solid #999;
      font-size: 11px;
    }

    .wf-arrow {
      font-size: 12px;
      color: #666;
    }

    /* Table */
    .table-wrapper {
      margin: 16px 0;
      overflow-x: auto;
    }

    table {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      font-size: 12px;
    }

    th, td {
      padding: 6px 12px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      text-align: left;
      vertical-align: top;
    }

    th {
      font-weight: normal;
      font-style: italic;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    }

    .table-caption {
      font-size: 11px;
      margin-top: 4px;
      margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    /* References */
    .references {
      font-size: 11px;
      line-height: 1.5;
    }

    .references p {
      text-indent: -2em;
      padding-left: 2em;
      margin-bottom: 4px;
    }

    /* Footer */
    .paper-footer {
      margin-top: 48px;
      padding-top: 12px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      font-size: 11px;
      text-align: center;
      color: var(--dim);
    }

    /* Horizontal rule */
    .hr {
      border: none;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      margin: 0 0 12px;
    }
  </style>

  <div class="paper">
    <div class="header">
      <div class="header-left">
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--dim);margin-bottom:12px;">Software Paper</div>
      </div>
      <div class="header-right">
        {new Date().toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long' })}<br>
        Submitted to:<br>
        <em>Journal of Statistical Software</em>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h1 class="paper-title">
      <em>Glade</em>: A Visual Desktop Environment for<br>
      Bayesian Statistical Workflow Management
    </h1>

    <p class="authors">
      The Glade Contributors
    </p>
    <p class="affiliation">
      Distributed under the MIT License
    </p>

    <div class="abstract">
      <p class="abstract-label">Abstract</p>
      <p>
        We introduce Glade, an open-source desktop application that provides a structured visual interface
        for Bayesian statistical modeling workflows. Built on the Bayesgrove R package, Glade represents
        each step of an analysis&thinsp;&mdash;&thinsp;from data loading through model fitting, diagnostic checking,
        and decision recording&thinsp;&mdash;&thinsp;as a connected node in an interactive workflow graph. The environment
        maintains a persistent connection to a live R session, surfaces protocol-aware validation
        obligations from the analysis state, and supports preview and execution of recommended actions
        with full parameter disclosure. We describe the design philosophy, system architecture, and
        intended use cases for researchers conducting Bayesian analyses in production settings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="keywords">
      <strong>Keywords:</strong> Bayesian inference, workflow management, R, graphical interface, reproducible research
    </p>

    <hr class="hr">

    <p class="section-heading">1. Introduction</p>

    <p class="body-text">
      Bayesian statistical analysis typically proceeds through a structured sequence of steps: data preparation,
      prior specification, model fitting, posterior diagnostics, sensitivity analysis, and decision-making.
      While each individual step is well-supported by existing R packages (e.g., <em>rstan</em>, <em>brms</em>,
      <em>cmdstanr</em>), the management of the overall workflow&thinsp;&mdash;&thinsp;tracking which steps have been
      completed, which validations remain outstanding, and how decisions at one stage affect downstream
      analysis&thinsp;&mdash;&thinsp;is largely left to the individual analyst.
    </p>

    <p class="body-text">
      Glade addresses this gap by providing a visual environment where the entire Bayesian analysis
      pipeline is represented as a directed graph. Each node in the graph corresponds to a discrete
      analytical operation, and edges encode the dependency relationships between operations. The
      environment integrates directly with the Bayesgrove package, which provides the underlying
      protocol for workflow state management, obligation tracking, and action recommendation.
    </p>

    <p class="section-heading">2. System Overview</p>

    <p class="body-text">
      Glade is implemented as a cross-platform desktop application using a web-based rendering layer
      (React) with a native shell (Electron). The application manages three primary interface surfaces:
      a workflow canvas, a terminal panel with an embedded R REPL, and protocol panels for obligation
      and action management.
    </p>

    <div class="figure">
      <div class="figure-box">
        <div class="workflow">
          <div class="wf-step">Data</div>
          <span class="wf-arrow">&rarr;</span>
          <div class="wf-step">Priors</div>
          <span class="wf-arrow">&rarr;</span>
          <div class="wf-step">Fit</div>
          <span class="wf-arrow">&rarr;</span>
          <div class="wf-step">Diagnose</div>
          <span class="wf-arrow">&rarr;</span>
          <div class="wf-step">Decide</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="figure-caption">
        <span class="fig-label">Figure 1.</span> Typical node progression in a Glade Bayesian workflow.
        Each node is connected by directed edges representing analytical dependencies.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="subsection-heading">2.1 Workflow Canvas</p>

    <p class="body-text">
      The canvas renders the analysis workflow as an interactive node graph using the XYFlow layout
      engine with ELK-based automatic positioning. Analysts can create, connect, annotate, and
      rearrange nodes to reflect their analytical pipeline. Each node stores metadata including
      the analysis stage, parameters, and execution results.
    </p>

    <p class="subsection-heading">2.2 Embedded R Session</p>

    <p class="body-text">
      A terminal panel provides direct access to the Bayesgrove R session via the xterm.js terminal
      emulator. The REPL supports command history, tab completion, and can be detached into a separate
      window for multi-monitor workflows. Process-level R output is available in a separate tab for
      debugging.
    </p>

    <p class="subsection-heading">2.3 Protocol Panels</p>

    <p class="body-text">
      Glade surfaces two types of protocol-driven interfaces. <em>Obligation panels</em> display
      blocking and advisory review tasks organized by protocol scope, with severity indicators
      and affected node references. <em>Action panels</em> present Bayesgrove-recommended operations
      with full parameter inspection, invocation fields, and template metadata before execution.
    </p>

    <p class="section-heading">3. Feature Summary</p>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <p class="table-caption"><span class="fig-label">Table 1.</span> Core capabilities of the Glade environment.</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Component</th>
            <th>Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Workflow Graph</td>
            <td>Interactive node-based canvas with ELK auto-layout, annotations, and multi-select.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>R REPL</td>
            <td>Live Bayesgrove session with command history, detached window, and raw output view.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Obligations</td>
            <td>Protocol-aware review tasks (blocking/advisory) surfaced from workflow state.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Actions</td>
            <td>Preview and execute Bayesgrove-recommended operations with parameter inspection.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Node Detail</td>
            <td>Inspection drawer for node metadata, decisions, records, and file attachments.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Desktop Bridge</td>
            <td>Native file picker, external editor launching, and R executable configuration.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="section-heading">4. Installation</p>

    <p class="body-text">
      Glade is distributed as a standalone desktop application for macOS, Linux, and Windows.
      The application requires a local R installation (version 4.3 or later) and the Bayesgrove
      package. Automatic updates are provided through built-in release channels.
    </p>

    <p class="section-heading">References</p>

    <div class="references">
      <p>[1] R Core Team (2024). <em>R: A Language and Environment for Statistical Computing</em>. R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria.</p>
      <p>[2] Gabry, J. et al. (2024). &ldquo;Bayesgrove: Protocol-Driven Bayesian Workflow Management.&rdquo; <em>Bayesian Analysis</em>, 1&ndash;25.</p>
      <p>[3] Carpenter, B. et al. (2017). &ldquo;Stan: A Probabilistic Programming Language.&rdquo; <em>Journal of Statistical Software</em>, 76(1), 1&ndash;32.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="paper-footer">
      Glade v0.16.2 &middot; MIT License &middot; {new Date().getFullYear()}
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
